RISC3D test kit 14 (prototype, 13 November 2026)
================================================

RISC3D 0.5: OpenGL ES 3.0/3.1 and OpenGL 3.0/3.1 now go to the GPU too,
with the rest of the extensions riscos-mesa offers in OpenGL 2.1. This kit
comes with the package that installs RISC3D (riscos-risc3d 0.5-1).

Needs
-----
 * RISC OS on Linux on the Pi 4 with riscos-kernel-pi 6.6.78-9 (the GPU's
   driver), as for the kits before.
 * A 16 million colour mode for the pictures and Overlay.

Steps
-----
 1. Copy RISC3DTest to HardDisc4 (unzip it there).
 2. In RiSH's Root shell:
      apt-get install --install-recommends /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/riscos-risc3d_0.5-1_arm64.deb
    (Debian's Mesa comes with it if it isn't there already.) Then restart:
    the modules load at start-up from now on. With riscos-support
    1.0.0049-1 or later the helper starts at start-up too; with an older
    one, start it after each restart (the install says how).
 3. In RiSH's Root shell:
      sh /riscos/HardDisc4/RISC3DTest/linux-tests
    It runs the same tests with Linux's Mesa directly, to compare with.
 4. Double-click GL3. GL3Results has two parts:
      es3: OpenGL ES 3.0/3.1 (uniform blocks, transform feedback, instanced
           and integer arrays, primitive restart, sync objects, a sampler,
           indirect draws, a compute shader, a blit).
      gl3: OpenGL 3.0/3.1. Linux's Mesa on the Pi gives OpenGL 3.1
           without the compatibility profile (2.1 with it), so this part
           should say "compatibility profile: no".
    Compare it with GL3LinuxResults: the lines after each part's version
    lines should be the same, pictures included.
 5. Double-click ES (as kit 13). ESResults has two parts:
      es1: OpenGL ES 1.1 (fixed-point matrices and arrays, lighting, the
           point size array, a vertex array object, a mapped buffer, an
           ETC1 texture). Linux's Mesa has no GLES 1.1, so RISC3D draws it
           on desktop OpenGL, with Mesa 20.3.5's own conversions: the
           numbers should be riscos-mesa's.
      es2: OpenGL ES 2's extensions (base vertex and instanced draws,
           vertex array objects, mapped ranges, separate programs, debug
           messages with a callback, 3D textures, border colours, queries,
           program binaries).
    Compare its es2 part with ESLinuxResults: the lines after the
    version should be the same, picture included.
 6. Cube, Test, GL and Overlay as before (check nothing went backwards).

Please send
-----------
GL3Results and GL3LinuxResults, ESResults and ESLinuxResults, and the
output of step 3 (a photo is fine). If anything stops, /riscos/3d/log.txt (the helper's log).
